## 1. Overview & Key Concepts

**RequireJS** is the JavaScript module loader used by Magento 2. It implements the **Asynchronous Module Definition (AMD)** specification, enabling:

- Lazy-loading of JavaScript files
- Explicit dependency declarations
- Modular, reusable JavaScript components

**Why it matters for Magento 2:**

| Concept | Purpose |
|---|---|
| AMD modules | Define self-contained JS with declared dependencies |
| `requirejs-config.js` | Central configuration per module/theme |
| Path aliases | Map short names to file locations |
| Module mixins | Extend/override existing JS modules without replacing them |
| Config merging | All configs across all modules/themes are combined at build time |

> **Exam focus:** Magento 2 uses RequireJS as its primary JavaScript module system. Know that it follows the AMD specification and that all `requirejs-config.js` files are *merged*, not overridden.

---

## 2. requirejs-config.js File Locations

The `requirejs-config.js` file can exist in **two key locations**:

### Module (Component) Location

```
app/code/Vendor/Module/view/frontend/requirejs-config.js
app/code/Vendor/Module/view/base/requirejs-config.js
app/code/Vendor/Module/view/adminhtml/requirejs-config.js
```

### Theme Location

```
app/design/frontend/Vendor/theme/requirejs-config.js
app/design/frontend/Vendor/theme/Vendor_Module/requirejs-config.js
```

### Visual Overview

```
Magento Root
|
+-- app/
|   +-- code/
|   |   +-- Vendor/Module/view/frontend/
|   |       +-- requirejs-config.js       <-- Module-level config
|   |
|   +-- design/
|       +-- frontend/Vendor/theme/
|           +-- requirejs-config.js       <-- Theme-level config
|           +-- Magento_Theme/
|               +-- requirejs-config.js   <-- Module-specific override in theme
|
+-- lib/
    +-- web/                              <-- Core JS library files
```

> **Exam focus:** A `requirejs-config.js` at the **theme root level** applies globally to that theme. A config inside `Vendor_Module/` within a theme applies only in the context of that module. Know both paths.

---

## 3. Configuration Sections Deep Dive

Every `requirejs-config.js` exports a single configuration object using this pattern:

```javascript
var config = {
    // configuration sections go here
};
```

The variable **must** be named `config` — Magento's framework wraps each file's content in an IIFE and passes `config` to `require.config()` automatically (see `Magento\Framework\RequireJs\Config::PARTIAL_CONFIG_TEMPLATE`).

---

### 3.1 paths — Alias to File Path

The `paths` section maps a **short module ID** (alias) to the actual **file path** (without `.js` extension).

```javascript
var config = {
    paths: {
        'myAlias': 'Vendor_Module/js/my-script',
        'chartjs':  'Vendor_Module/js/vendor/chart.min',
        'slick':    'js/slick/slick'
    }
};
```

**How paths are resolved:**
- Paths are **relative to the RequireJS `baseUrl`**, which Magento sets to the deployed static content directory for the active theme/locale.
- Use **module notation** (`Vendor_Module/js/file`) — NOT absolute URLs or server-relative paths.

**Real Magento example** (from `module-theme/view/base/requirejs-config.js`):

```javascript
paths: {
    'jquery/validate': 'jquery/jquery.validate',
    'jquery/uppy-core': 'jquery/uppy/dist/uppy.min',
    'text': 'mage/requirejs/text',
    'domReady': 'requirejs/domReady',
    'spectrum': 'jquery/spectrum/spectrum',
    'jquery-ui-modules': 'jquery/ui-modules'
}
```

```javascript
// CORRECT - module notation
paths: {
    'myLib': 'Vendor_Module/js/my-lib'
}

// CORRECT - relative path from baseUrl (lib/web)
paths: {
    'jquery-ui': 'jquery/ui'
}

// WRONG - absolute path (will break)
paths: {
    'myLib': '/pub/static/frontend/Vendor/theme/js/my-lib'
}

// WRONG - .js extension included (RequireJS adds it automatically)
paths: {
    'myLib': 'Vendor_Module/js/my-lib.js'
}
```

> **Exam focus:** Never use absolute paths in the `paths` config. Do NOT include the `.js` extension. Use module notation (`Vendor_Module/js/file`) or paths relative to `baseUrl`.

---

### 3.2 map — Remap Module IDs

The `map` section **replaces one module ID with another** — globally or for specific requiring modules.

```javascript
var config = {
    map: {
        '*': {
            'target/module': 'replacement/module'
        }
    }
};
```

**The `*` wildcard** means the remapping applies to **all modules** — i.e., any module that requires `'target/module'` will get `'replacement/module'` instead.

**Scoped mapping** — only applies when a specific module does the requiring:

```javascript
var config = {
    map: {
        // Only when 'some/module' requires 'target/module', give it 'special/version'
        'some/module': {
            'target/module': 'special/version'
        },
        // For everyone else
        '*': {
            'target/module': 'standard/version'
        }
    }
};
```

**Real-world Magento example** (from `module-theme/view/base/requirejs-config.js`):

```javascript
var config = {
    map: {
        '*': {
            'ko': 'knockoutjs/knockout',
            'knockout': 'knockoutjs/knockout',
            'mageUtils': 'mage/utils/main',
            'rjsResolver': 'mage/requirejs/resolver'
        }
    }
};
```

> **Note:** `ko` and `knockout` are both mapped to `knockoutjs/knockout` via `map`, NOT `paths`. This is a module ID → module ID remapping.

> **Exam focus:** `map: { "*": { "old": "new" } }` is the pattern for **globally replacing** a module. This is different from `paths` — `map` redirects a module ID to another module ID, while `paths` maps an ID to a file location.

**map vs paths comparison:**

| Feature | `paths` | `map` |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Alias ID → file location | Remap ID → different module ID |
| Scope | Global | Per-requirer or global (`*`) |
| Use case | Shorthand for long paths | Module substitution/override |
| Targets | File paths | Module IDs |

---

### 3.3 shim — Non-AMD Libraries

The `shim` section handles **legacy JavaScript libraries** that do not use AMD `define()`. It tells RequireJS:
- What **dependencies** to load first
- What **global variable** the library exports

```javascript
var config = {
    shim: {
        'legacy-plugin': {
            deps: ['jquery'],
            exports: 'LegacyPlugin'
        },
        'some-jquery-plugin': {
            deps: ['jquery']
            // No exports needed if it just attaches to $
        }
    }
};
```

**Real Magento examples** (from the codebase):

```javascript
// module-theme/view/base/requirejs-config.js
shim: {
    'jquery/ui': ['jquery'],                    // shorthand: deps-only array
    'matchMedia': { 'exports': 'mediaCheck' },
    'vimeo/player': { 'exports': 'Player' }
}

// module-ui/view/base/requirejs-config.js — uses init property
shim: {
    'hugerte/hugerte.min': {
        exports: 'hugerte',
        init: function () {
            'use strict';
            window.tinymce = window.hugerte;
            window.tinyMCE = window.hugerte;
            return window.hugerte;
        }
    }
}
```

**Key shim properties:**

| Property | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `deps` | Optional | Array of dependencies to load before this module |
| `exports` | Optional | Global variable name this library creates |
| `init` | Optional | Function to run after load; return value used as module value |

> **Exam focus:** Use `shim` for libraries that are NOT written as AMD modules (no `define()` call). If a library already uses `define()`, shimming it is unnecessary and can cause issues. Shorthand: `{ 'module': ['dep1', 'dep2'] }` is equivalent to `{ 'module': { deps: ['dep1', 'dep2'] } }`.

---

### 3.4 deps — Auto-Load Dependencies

The `deps` section specifies modules that should be **loaded immediately** when RequireJS initializes — before any explicit `require()` call.

```javascript
var config = {
    deps: [
        'Vendor_Module/js/my-init-script',
        'Vendor_Module/js/polyfills'
    ]
};
```

**Real Magento example** (from `module-theme/view/frontend/requirejs-config.js`):

```javascript
deps: [
    'mage/common',
    'mage/dataPost',
    'mage/bootstrap'
],
```

**Use cases:**
- Loading polyfills that must be present before anything else
- Auto-initializing widgets that operate on the page globally
- Loading tracking/analytics scripts

> **Exam focus:** `deps` at the top level of the config object auto-loads modules on page load. This is different from `deps` inside a `shim` entry (which specifies prerequisites for a specific shimmed library).

---

### 3.5 config > mixins — Module Mixins

The `mixins` section (under `config`) lets you **extend existing AMD modules** without replacing them. The mixin function receives the original module and returns a modified version.

```javascript
var config = {
    config: {
        mixins: {
            'Magento_Checkout/js/model/quote': {
                'Vendor_Module/js/model/quote-mixin': true
            },
            'Magento_Catalog/js/price-box': {
                'Vendor_Module/js/price-box-mixin': true
            }
        }
    }
};
```

**Real Magento example** (from `module-theme/view/frontend/requirejs-config.js`):

```javascript
config: {
    mixins: {
        'Magento_Theme/js/view/breadcrumbs': {
            'Magento_Theme/js/view/add-home-breadcrumb': true
        }
    }
}
```

**Mixin for a uiComponent (has `.extend()` method):**

```javascript
// Vendor_Module/js/model/some-ui-component-mixin.js
define([], function () {
    'use strict';

    return function (originalComponent) {
        // uiComponents have .extend() for class-level extension
        return originalComponent.extend({
            myNewMethod: function () {
                // new functionality
            }
        });
    };
});
```

> **Note:** `.extend()` is available on uiComponent-based modules (those inheriting from `Magento_Ui/js/lib/core/element/element`). Plain objects (like `Magento_Checkout/js/model/quote`) do NOT have `.extend()` — for those, modify the target object directly or use `mage/utils/wrapper`.

**Mixin for a jQuery widget:**

```javascript
// Vendor_Module/js/price-box-mixin.js
define(['jquery'], function ($) {
    'use strict';

    var mixin = {
        _create: function () {
            // Custom logic before or after calling original
            console.log('Price box mixin: _create called');
            this._super(); // calls original _create
        }
    };

    return function (targetWidget) {
        $.widget('mage.priceBox', targetWidget, mixin);
        return $.mage.priceBox;
    };
});
```

> **Corrected:** The jQuery widget mixin MUST import `jquery` in its dependency array — `define(['jquery'], function ($) { ... })`. Without it, `$.widget()` and `$.mage.priceBox` are undefined. `this._super()` calls the original method from the parent widget.

> **Exam focus:** Mixins live under `config: { mixins: { ... } }` — note the nested structure. The value `true` enables the mixin. Setting it to `false` disables it. Mixins are the **preferred** way to extend JS functionality in Magento 2 without overriding files.

---

## 4. AMD Module Definition: define()

The `define()` function creates a **reusable AMD module** that can be required by other code.

### Syntax

```javascript
define([dependencies], factory);
```

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `[dependencies]` | Array (optional) | Module IDs this module depends on |
| `factory` | Function or Object | Called with resolved deps; return value = module |

### Basic Module (No Dependencies)

```javascript
// Vendor_Module/view/frontend/web/js/my-module.js
define([], function () {
    'use strict';

    return {
        greet: function (name) {
            return 'Hello, ' + name + '!';
        }
    };
});
```

### Module With Dependencies

```javascript
// Vendor_Module/view/frontend/web/js/form-handler.js
define([
    'jquery',
    'Magento_Customer/js/model/customer',
    'mage/url'
], function ($, customer, urlBuilder) {
    'use strict';

    return {
        submitForm: function (formData) {
            var url = urlBuilder.build('vendor/module/save');
            return $.ajax({
                url: url,
                method: 'POST',
                data: formData
            });
        },
        isLoggedIn: function () {
            return customer.isLoggedIn();
        }
    };
});
```

> **Corrected:** Changed `customer.customerId()` to `customer.isLoggedIn()`. The `Magento_Customer/js/model/customer` module exposes `customerData`, `isLoggedIn` (KO observable), `setIsLoggedIn`, `getBillingAddressList`, `getShippingAddressList` — but NOT `customerId`. Customer ID is in `customerData` (sourced from `window.customerData`).

### Returning a Constructor Function

```javascript
define(['jquery'], function ($) {
    'use strict';

    function MyWidget(element, options) {
        this.element = $(element);
        this.options = options || {};
        this._init();
    }

    MyWidget.prototype._init = function () {
        this.element.on('click', this._onClick.bind(this));
    };

    MyWidget.prototype._onClick = function (e) {
        e.preventDefault();
        console.log('Clicked:', this.options.message);
    };

    return MyWidget;
});
```

### Named Modules (Avoid in Magento)

```javascript
// Named define — generally AVOID in Magento
define('my/module/name', ['jquery'], function ($) {
    // ...
});
```

> **Exam focus:** In Magento 2, modules should almost always be **anonymous** (no name as first argument). Named modules are hard-coded to a specific ID and cannot be remapped by `paths` or `map` config.

---

## 5. Consuming Modules: require()

The `require()` function **loads modules** and executes a callback once they're ready. It is used for **consumption**, not definition.

### Syntax

```javascript
require([dependencies], callback, errback);
```

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `[dependencies]` | Array | Module IDs to load |
| `callback` | Function | Executed when all deps are loaded |
| `errback` | Function (optional) | Executed if loading fails |

### Basic Usage

```javascript
require(['jquery', 'Vendor_Module/js/my-module'], function ($, myModule) {
    'use strict';

    $(document).ready(function () {
        var result = myModule.greet('World');
        console.log(result); // "Hello, World!"
    });
});
```

### With Error Handling

```javascript
require(
    ['Vendor_Module/js/heavy-component'],
    function (HeavyComponent) {
        var instance = new HeavyComponent('#target');
        instance.init();
    },
    function (err) {
        console.error('Failed to load module:', err);
    }
);
```

### Inline require() Inside define()

```javascript
define(['require', 'jquery'], function (require, $) {
    'use strict';

    return {
        loadOnDemand: function () {
            // Lazy-load a module only when needed
            require(['Vendor_Module/js/lazy-component'], function (LazyComp) {
                LazyComp.init();
            });
        }
    };
});
```

### define() vs require() — Key Differences

| | `define()` | `require()` |
|---|---|---|
| **Purpose** | Creates/exports a module | Consumes modules |
| **Returns** | Module value (via return) | Nothing (uses callback) |
| **Usage** | In module files | In entry-point scripts |
| **Reusable** | Yes — can be required elsewhere | No — executes immediately |
| **AMD spec** | Defines a module | Triggers module loading |

> **Exam focus:** `define()` is for **creating** modules (in `.js` files). `require()` is for **using** modules (typically inline scripts or entry-point files). A file containing `define()` does NOT execute until it is required.

---

## 6. Module ID Resolution & Fallback Order

When RequireJS resolves a module ID, Magento applies a **fallback chain**. This allows themes to override module-level JS files.

### Fallback Order (Highest to Lowest Priority)

```
1. Current Theme
       |
       v
2. Parent Theme(s)   [if theme inheritance is configured]
       |
       v
3. Module (app/code/Vendor/Module/view/frontend/web/)
       |
       v
4. lib/web/          [Magento core JS libraries]
```

### How Module IDs Map to File Paths

| Module ID | Resolves To | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| `jquery` | `lib/web/jquery.js` | Direct file path (jquery.js exists in deployed static dir) |
| `ko` | `lib/web/knockoutjs/knockout.js` | Via `map: { '*': { 'ko': 'knockoutjs/knockout' } }` |
| `Vendor_Module/js/file` | `Vendor/Module/view/frontend/web/js/file.js` | Module notation |
| `mage/utils` | `lib/web/mage/utils.js` | Direct file path |
| `mageUtils` | `lib/web/mage/utils/main.js` | Via `map: { '*': { 'mageUtils': 'mage/utils/main' } }` |
| `myAlias` | Whatever `paths.myAlias` points to | Via `paths` config |

> **Corrected:** `jquery` resolves via direct file path resolution (the file `jquery.js` exists in the deployed static directory), NOT via a `paths` alias. `ko` resolves via `map` (not `paths`). The distinction between `map` and `paths` resolution matters for the exam.

### Theme Override Example

```
app/design/frontend/Vendor/MyTheme/
    Magento_Catalog/
        web/
            js/
                product.js   <-- This OVERRIDES the module's product.js
```

When any code requires `Magento_Catalog/js/product`, Magento's fallback serves the theme version first.

> **Exam focus:** The fallback order is **Theme → Parent Theme → Module → lib/web**. A file in the theme's `Vendor_Module/web/js/` directory overrides the module's JS file without any configuration change needed.

---

## 7. Config Merging Across Modules & Themes

This is one of the **most tested and most misunderstood** concepts.

### The Rule

> All `requirejs-config.js` files across **all enabled modules** and the **active theme** are **collected and merged** into a single configuration object at page load time.

### Merge Order

The merge order is controlled by `Magento\Framework\RequireJs\Config\File\Collector\Aggregated`:

```
1. Library files (lib/web)
       |
       v
2. Module configs (all enabled modules, ordered by sequence)
       |
       v
3. Theme modular configs (Vendor_Module/requirejs-config.js inside theme)
       |
       v
4. Theme root config (theme's own requirejs-config.js)
       |
       v
Final merged config
```

**Later configs extend (but do not completely replace) earlier ones.**

### Practical Merging Behavior

```javascript
// Module A's requirejs-config.js
var config = {
    paths: {
        'myLib': 'ModuleA/js/my-lib'
    },
    shim: {
        'legacy': { deps: ['jquery'] }
    }
};

// Theme's requirejs-config.js (loaded AFTER Module A)
var config = {
    paths: {
        'myLib': 'Vendor_Theme/js/my-lib',  // Overrides Module A's path
        'newLib': 'Vendor_Theme/js/new-lib'  // Adds new entry
    }
};

// Final merged result:
// paths: {
//     'myLib': 'Vendor_Theme/js/my-lib',  <-- theme wins
//     'newLib': 'Vendor_Theme/js/new-lib'
// }
// shim: {
//     'legacy': { deps: ['jquery'] }       <-- still present from Module A
// }
```

### Merging Mixins (Additive)

Multiple modules can add mixins to the same target — they **all apply**:

```javascript
// Module A
var config = {
    config: {
        mixins: {
            'Magento_Checkout/js/model/quote': {
                'ModuleA/js/quote-mixin': true
            }
        }
    }
};

// Module B (both mixins will be applied)
var config = {
    config: {
        mixins: {
            'Magento_Checkout/js/model/quote': {
                'ModuleB/js/quote-mixin': true
            }
        }
    }
};
```

> **Exam focus:** `requirejs-config.js` files are **MERGED**, not overridden. A theme's config **extends** module configs. For `paths`, the **last writer wins** for a given key. For `mixins`, all mixins for a given target are **additive** — they all apply.

---

## 8. Common Pitfalls & Best Practices

### Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Problem | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Absolute paths in `paths` | Breaks in different environments | Use module notation or baseUrl-relative paths |
| Including `.js` extension | RequireJS double-appends it | Remove `.js` from all module IDs |
| Using `require()` instead of `define()` in module files | Creates a non-reusable side-effect | Use `define()` for any file meant to be a module |
| Named `define()` calls | ID becomes fixed, can't be remapped | Use anonymous `define()` |
| Shimming AMD modules | Causes double-execution issues | Only shim non-AMD legacy libs |
| Assuming config files override each other | Missing features from earlier configs | Remember: configs MERGE |

### Best Practices

```javascript
// GOOD: Anonymous define with clear dependency list
define([
    'jquery',
    'ko',
    'Magento_Ui/js/lib/core/element/element'
], function ($, ko, Element) {
    'use strict';

    return Element.extend({
        // ...
    });
});

// GOOD: Module notation in paths
var config = {
    paths: {
        'slick': 'Vendor_Module/js/vendor/slick'
    }
};

// GOOD: map for module substitution
var config = {
    map: {
        '*': {
            'Magento_Ui/js/form/element/select': 'Vendor_Module/js/form/element/select'
        }
    }
};
```

> **Exam focus:** The correct way to replace a module globally is `map: { "*": { ... } }`. The correct way to extend a module (non-destructively) is `config: { mixins: { ... } }`. Know when to use each.

