## 9. Hands-On: Adding a Path Alias in a Theme

### Step 1: Create the JavaScript File

```bash
mkdir -p app/design/frontend/Vendor/MyTheme/web/js
```

```javascript
// app/design/frontend/Vendor/MyTheme/web/js/my-greeting.js
define([], function () {
    'use strict';

    return {
        sayHello: function (name) {
            return 'Hello from MyTheme, ' + name + '!';
        },
        sayGoodbye: function (name) {
            return 'Goodbye, ' + name + '!';
        }
    };
});
```

### Step 2: Create requirejs-config.js in the Theme

```javascript
// app/design/frontend/Vendor/MyTheme/requirejs-config.js
var config = {
    paths: {
        'myGreeting': 'js/my-greeting'
        // Resolves to {baseUrl}/js/my-greeting.js
        // After static deploy: pub/static/frontend/Vendor/MyTheme/{locale}/js/my-greeting.js
    }
};
```

> **Corrected:** For theme-level files in `web/js/`, use paths relative to baseUrl: `'js/my-greeting'`. The baseUrl already includes the theme path after static content deployment. Do NOT use `'Vendor_MyTheme/js/my-greeting'` — themes are not addressed via module notation like modules are.

### Step 3: Deploy Static Content & Clear Cache

```bash
# Clear generated files
rm -rf generated/ var/cache/ var/page_cache/ var/view_preprocessed/

# Deploy static content (developer mode doesn't need full deploy)
php bin/magento setup:static-content:deploy -f

# Or in developer mode, just clear cache
php bin/magento cache:flush
```

### Step 4: Verify in Browser Console

Open your browser's Developer Tools console and test:

```javascript
// Test 1: Load the module by alias
require(['myGreeting'], function (greeting) {
    console.log(greeting.sayHello('Magento Dev'));
    // Expected: "Hello from MyTheme, Magento Dev!"
});

// Test 2: Inspect the merged RequireJS config
console.log(require.s.contexts._.config.paths);
// Should show 'myGreeting' entry in the paths object

// Test 3: Check what file was loaded
console.log(require.s.contexts._.defined['myGreeting']);
// Should show the module object
```

### Step 5: Use the Alias in Another Module

```javascript
// app/design/frontend/Vendor/MyTheme/web/js/page-init.js
define(['myGreeting', 'jquery'], function (greeting, $) {
    'use strict';

    return function (config, element) {
        var message = greeting.sayHello(config.customerName || 'Guest');
        $(element).text(message);
    };
});
```

```xml
<!-- Layout XML to load the script -->
<page xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
      xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:View/Layout/etc/page_configuration.xsd">
    <body>
        <referenceContainer name="content">
            <block class="Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template"
                   name="my.greeting.block"
                   template="Vendor_MyTheme::greeting.phtml"/>
        </referenceContainer>
    </body>
</page>
```

```php
<?php
// Vendor_MyTheme/templates/greeting.phtml
/** @var \Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template $block */
?>
<div id="my-greeting"
     data-mage-init='{"js/page-init": {"customerName": "John"}}'>
</div>
```

> **Corrected:** The `data-mage-init` value uses `"js/page-init"` (relative to baseUrl) since this is a theme-level file, not a module file. For module files you would use `"Vendor_Module/js/page-init"`.

### Expected Browser Console Output

```
Hello from MyTheme, John!
```

### Troubleshooting Checklist

```
[ ] Cache flushed after adding requirejs-config.js
[ ] Static content deployed (production mode)
[ ] No .js extension in paths value
[ ] Variable named exactly 'config' in requirejs-config.js
[ ] File exists at the path the alias points to
[ ] Browser hard-refresh to bypass browser cache (Ctrl+Shift+R)
[ ] Check Network tab for 404s on JS files
[ ] Check Console for RequireJS timeout errors
```

