## 2. Knockout.js (KO) in Magento 2

### 2.1 Why Knockout?

Magento 2 uses **Knockout.js** (KO) as its MVVM framework for dynamic frontend rendering, especially in:
- **Checkout** (entire multi-step checkout is KO-driven)
- **Shopping Cart** (mini-cart, cart page)
- **UI Components** (admin grids, forms)

KO uses **declarative bindings** in HTML (`data-bind`) and **observables** in JS to create a two-way data binding between the view and the view model.

```
ViewModel (JS)         View (HTML Template)
+-----------+          +------------------+
| name obs  | <------> | data-bind="text: |
| click fn  |          |   name"          |
+-----------+          +------------------+
   Updates automatically when observable changes
```

---

### 2.2 Observables

An **observable** is a special variable that automatically notifies subscribers when its value changes, triggering a UI re-render.

```javascript
define(['ko'], function (ko) {
    'use strict';

    return {
        // Declaration: wrap initial value in ko.observable()
        firstName: ko.observable('John'),
        lastName: ko.observable('Doe'),
        isLoggedIn: ko.observable(false),
        quantity: ko.observable(1)
    };
});
```

**Getting and setting values — THE most critical rule:**

```javascript
// GETTING a value: call it as a FUNCTION with no arguments
var name = this.firstName();        // Returns 'John'
var qty = this.quantity();          // Returns 1

// SETTING a value: call it as a FUNCTION with the new value as argument
this.firstName('Jane');             // Sets to 'Jane', triggers UI update
this.quantity(5);                   // Sets to 5, triggers UI update

// WRONG — NEVER do this:
this.firstName = 'Jane';            // BAD! Replaces the observable with a string!
this.quantity = 5;                  // BAD! Destroys the observable!
```

> **Exam focus:** This is **explicitly highlighted** in the exam objectives as "tricky." Observable values are **called as functions** — `this.name()` to get, `this.name('value')` to set. Using `=` assignment **replaces the observable itself** with a plain value, breaking all bindings. This is one of the most common KO mistakes.

**Subscribing to observable changes:**

```javascript
this.firstName.subscribe(function (newValue) {
    console.log('firstName changed to: ' + newValue);
});
```

---

### 2.3 Computed Observables

A **computed observable** automatically recalculates whenever any observable it depends on changes.

```javascript
define(['ko'], function (ko) {
    'use strict';

    var ViewModel = function () {
        this.firstName = ko.observable('John');
        this.lastName = ko.observable('Doe');

        // Computed: automatically updates when firstName or lastName changes
        this.fullName = ko.computed(function () {
            return this.firstName() + ' ' + this.lastName();
        }, this); // 'this' context passed as second argument

        // Writable computed
        this.fullNameWritable = ko.pureComputed({
            read: function () {
                return this.firstName() + ' ' + this.lastName();
            },
            write: function (value) {
                var parts = value.split(' ');
                this.firstName(parts[0]);
                this.lastName(parts[1] || '');
            },
            owner: this
        });
    };
});
```

> **Exam focus:** `ko.computed()` requires a **second argument for `this` context** when using `this` inside the function body. Omitting it causes `this` to be undefined/window. `ko.pureComputed()` is a memory-efficient alternative for read-only computeds.

---

### 2.4 Observable Arrays

```javascript
define(['ko'], function (ko) {
    'use strict';

    var ViewModel = function () {
        this.cartItems = ko.observableArray([]);       // Empty array
        this.categories = ko.observableArray(['a','b']); // Pre-populated

        // Array manipulation (each triggers UI update)
        this.cartItems.push({ name: 'Item 1', price: 10 });
        this.cartItems.pop();
        this.cartItems.remove(function (item) {
            return item.price < 5;
        });
        this.cartItems.removeAll();

        // Getting the underlying array
        var items = this.cartItems(); // Returns plain JS array
        var count = this.cartItems().length;
    };
});
```

> **Exam focus:** `ko.observableArray` mutations (`.push()`, `.pop()`, `.remove()`) are called on the observable directly (not on the unwrapped array). Calling `.push()` on `this.cartItems()` would push to the plain array without triggering updates.

---

### 2.5 Data Bindings

Data bindings are declared in HTML templates using `data-bind` attributes:

```html
<!-- Text binding: displays observable value as text -->
<span data-bind="text: fullName"></span>

<!-- HTML binding: renders HTML content (XSS risk!) -->
<div data-bind="html: htmlContent"></div>

<!-- Value binding: two-way binding for form inputs -->
<input type="text" data-bind="value: firstName" />

<!-- Click binding: calls a function on click -->
<button data-bind="click: submitForm">Submit</button>

<!-- Event binding: bind to any DOM event -->
<input data-bind="event: { keyup: onKeyUp, blur: onBlur }" />

<!-- Visible binding: show/hide based on observable -->
<div data-bind="visible: isLoggedIn">Welcome back!</div>

<!-- CSS binding: conditionally apply CSS classes -->
<div data-bind="css: { 'active': isActive, 'disabled': isDisabled }"></div>

<!-- Attr binding: set HTML attributes dynamically -->
<a data-bind="attr: { href: productUrl, title: productName }">Link</a>

<!-- Foreach binding: iterate over observable array -->
<ul data-bind="foreach: cartItems">
    <li>
        <!-- Inside foreach, context is the array item -->
        <span data-bind="text: name"></span>
        <span data-bind="text: price"></span>
    </li>
</ul>

<!-- If/ifnot binding: conditional rendering (removes from DOM) -->
<div data-bind="if: isLoggedIn">
    <span>User is logged in</span>
</div>
<div data-bind="ifnot: isLoggedIn">
    <span>Please log in</span>
</div>

<!-- With binding: change data context -->
<div data-bind="with: selectedProduct">
    <span data-bind="text: name"></span>
</div>

<!-- Component binding: load a UI component -->
<div data-bind="component: { name: 'my-component', params: { data: items } }"></div>
```

**Binding context variables:**

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `$data` | Current context object |
| `$root` | Root ViewModel |
| `$parent` | Parent context (one level up) |
| `$parents[0]` | Same as `$parent` |
| `$parents[1]` | Two levels up |
| `$index` | Current index in `foreach` (observable) |
| `$element` | The DOM element |

```html
<!-- Using context variables -->
<ul data-bind="foreach: cartItems">
    <li>
        <span data-bind="text: $index() + 1"></span>  <!-- 1-based index -->
        <span data-bind="text: name"></span>
        <button data-bind="click: $parent.removeItem">Remove</button>
    </li>
</ul>
```

> **Exam focus:** `$index` inside `foreach` is itself an **observable** — call `$index()` not `$index` to get the numeric value.

---

### 2.6 Custom Binding Handlers

Custom bindings extend KO with new `data-bind` capabilities:

```javascript
define(['ko'], function (ko) {
    'use strict';

    // Custom binding: 'fadeVisible'
    ko.bindingHandlers.fadeVisible = {
        /**
         * Called once when binding is first applied
         * @param {Element} element - The DOM element
         * @param {Function} valueAccessor - Function returning the binding value
         * @param {Object} allBindings - All other bindings on this element
         * @param {Object} viewModel - The current ViewModel (deprecated, use bindingContext)
         * @param {Object} bindingContext - The full binding context ($root, $parent, etc.)
         */
        init: function (element, valueAccessor, allBindings, viewModel, bindingContext) {
            // Set initial visibility without animation
            var value = ko.unwrap(valueAccessor());
            $(element).toggle(value);
        },

        /**
         * Called whenever the binding value changes
         */
        update: function (element, valueAccessor) {
            var value = ko.unwrap(valueAccessor());
            if (value) {
                $(element).fadeIn();
            } else {
                $(element).fadeOut();
            }
        }
    };

    // Usage in HTML:
    // <div data-bind="fadeVisible: isVisible">Content</div>
});
```

**Key points for custom binding handlers:**
- `init` runs **once** when first applied
- `update` runs **every time** the bound observable changes (and also once on init)
- `ko.unwrap(valueAccessor())` safely gets the value whether it's an observable or plain value
- Register on `ko.bindingHandlers` object

> **Exam focus:** Both `init` and `update` receive `valueAccessor` as a **function** — you must **call** `valueAccessor()` to get the actual value. Using `valueAccessor` directly gives you the function, not the value.

