## 3. UI Component Architecture

### 3.1 Three Pillars of a UI Component

Every Magento 2 UI component consists of three interconnected pieces:

```
+---------------------------+
|   1. JavaScript Component  |  Defines behavior, observables, methods
|   (uiElement/uiCollection) |
+---------------------------+
            |
            | configured by
            v
+---------------------------+
|   2. XML Configuration    |  Declares component tree, arguments, data source
|   (ui_component XML file) |
+---------------------------+
            |
            | fetches data from
            v
+---------------------------+
|   3. Data Provider        |  PHP class providing data to JS via Ajax/JSON
|   (DataProvider PHP class)|  (mainly for admin grids/forms)
+---------------------------+
```

> **Exam focus:** The three pillars are JS component, XML config, and PHP data provider. Knowing which file type handles which responsibility is testable.

---

### 3.2 uiElement and uiCollection Hierarchy

```
uiClass  (base class — Magento_Ui/js/lib/core/class)
  |
  +-- uiElement   (single component, no children — Magento_Ui/js/lib/core/element/element)
        |
        +-- uiCollection  (manages child components — Magento_Ui/js/lib/core/collection)
              |
              Aliased as "uiComponent" in requirejs-config.js (most common base class)
              |
              +-- Form
              +-- Listing (Grid)
              +-- Modal
              +-- etc.
```

> **Exam focus:** `uiComponent` is an alias for `uiCollection` (NOT `uiElement`). In `Magento_Ui/view/base/requirejs-config.js`: `'uiComponent': 'Magento_Ui/js/lib/core/collection'`. This means any component extending `uiComponent` automatically gets child-management capabilities (`elems` observable array, etc.).

**`uiElement` key features:**
- Has `defaults` object for declaring default properties
- Has `links`, `imports`, `exports`, `listens` for component communication
- Has `initialize()`, `initObservable()`, `initConfig()` lifecycle methods
- Properties declared in `defaults.tracks` become KO observables automatically

**`uiCollection` key features:**
- Extends `uiElement`
- Manages a `children` collection
- `elems` is an observable array of child component instances
- Handles dynamic child component loading

```javascript
// Example uiElement-based component
define([
    'Magento_Ui/js/lib/core/element/element'
], function (Element) {
    'use strict';

    return Element.extend({
        defaults: {
            template: 'Vendor_Module/my-component',
            myValue: '',   // Will become observable if listed in tracks

            // Tracks: makes these properties KO observables automatically
            tracks: {
                myValue: true,
                isActive: true
            },

            // Links: two-way sync between this property and another component's property
            links: {
                value: '${ $.provider }:data.value'
            },

            // Imports: one-way sync from another component TO this component
            imports: {
                totalCount: '${ $.provider }:data.totalCount'
            },

            // Exports: one-way sync FROM this component to another
            exports: {
                currentPage: '${ $.provider }:params.current_page'
            }
        },

        initialize: function () {
            this._super();
            // Custom initialization
            return this;
        },

        initObservable: function () {
            this._super()
                .track({
                    manuallyTrackedProp: true
                });
            return this;
        }
    });
});
```

> **Exam focus:** Properties listed in `defaults.tracks` are **automatically converted to KO observables** by `uiElement`. You do NOT need to manually call `ko.observable()` for tracked properties.

> **Exam focus:** The `initialize()` method **must return `this`** at the end for proper chaining behavior.

---

