## 1. jQuery Widget Pattern Overview

Magento 2 uses the **jQuery UI Widget Factory** as its standard mechanism for encapsulating JavaScript component behavior. Every front-end component (sliders, accordions, dropdowns, minicarts) is built on this pattern.

### Core Widget Declaration Syntax

```javascript
$.widget("mage.widgetName", {

    // Default configuration options (overridable on init)
    options: {
        someOption: "defaultValue",
        count: 0,
        enabled: true
    },

    // Called ONCE when the widget is first created
    _create: function () {
        console.log("Widget created for element:", this.element);
        this._bind();
    },

    // Called on creation AND every subsequent re-initialization
    _init: function () {
        console.log("Widget initialized/re-initialized");
        this._render();
    },

    // Public method (callable externally)
    methodName: function () {
        // Access options via this.options
        var opt = this.options.someOption;
        // Access the wrapped DOM element via this.element
        this.element.text(opt);
    },

    // Private methods: prefix with underscore (convention)
    _bind: function () {
        this._on(this.element, {
            "click": this._handleClick
        });
    },

    _handleClick: function (event) {
        event.preventDefault();
        this.methodName();
    },

    _render: function () {
        // Rendering logic
    }
});
```

### Widget Namespace Convention

```
$.widget("namespace.widgetName", {...})
          |         |
          |         +-- camelCase widget name
          +-- mage (core Magento widgets)
              OR
              vendorName (custom widgets)
```

- **Exam focus:** The namespace prefix (`mage.`) is not decorative — it defines the jQuery plugin name. `mage.accordion` becomes callable as `$(el).accordion()`.

---

## 2. Widget Lifecycle Methods

Understanding the difference between `_create` and `_init` is a **frequently tested concept**.

```
Widget first bound to element
            |
            v
    +---------------+
    |   _create()   |  <-- Runs ONCE only, ever
    +---------------+
            |
            v
    +---------------+
    |    _init()    |  <-- Runs after _create, AND on every re-init
    +---------------+
            |
            v
    Widget is "live" and responding to events

Later, if widget is re-initialized (options change, etc.):

    +---------------+
    |    _init()    |  <-- Runs AGAIN (no _create)
    +---------------+
```

| Method | When It Runs | Typical Use |
|---|---|---|
| `_create` | **Once**, at widget instantiation | DOM manipulation, event binding, one-time setup |
| `_init` | At creation + **every re-initialization** | State reset, rendering based on current options |
| `_destroy` | When widget is destroyed | Clean up events, remove DOM nodes added by widget |
| `_setOption` | When a single option changes | React to runtime option updates |

```javascript
$.widget("mage.exampleWidget", {

    options: {
        message: "Hello"
    },

    _create: function () {
        // Runs once — set up your listeners here
        this._on(this.element, {
            "click .my-button": "_onButtonClick"
        });
        this._setupDOM(); // One-time DOM construction
    },

    _init: function () {
        // Runs every time — safe for rendering/state
        this.element.find(".message").text(this.options.message);
    },

    _setOption: function (key, value) {
        this._super(key, value); // Important: call parent!
        if (key === "message") {
            this._init(); // Re-render when message changes
        }
    },

    _onButtonClick: function (event) {
        event.preventDefault();
        this.toggleMessage();
    },

    toggleMessage: function () {
        this.element.find(".message").toggle();
    }
});
```

- **Exam focus:** `_create` runs **once**. `_init` runs on creation **and** re-initialization. If you put event binding in `_init`, you risk binding the same listener multiple times on re-init — always bind events in `_create`.
- **Exam focus:** Always call `this._super(key, value)` inside `_setOption` to ensure the jQuery UI option store is updated before your custom logic runs.

---

## 3. Widget Initialization Methods

Magento provides **two declarative HTML-based approaches** to initialize widgets, plus the programmatic jQuery approach.

### Method A: Programmatic (JavaScript)

```javascript
// Direct jQuery plugin call (after widget is defined)
require(["Vendor_Module/js/myWidget"], function () {
    $("#my-element").myWidget({
        someOption: "customValue"
    });
});
```

### Method B: `data-mage-init` Attribute

Inline HTML attribute — associates a widget with **the element itself**.

```html
<div id="my-element"
     data-mage-init='{"Vendor_Module/js/myWidget": {"someOption": "customValue"}}'>
    Widget content here
</div>
```

### Method C: `x-magento-init` Script Tag

A special `<script>` tag — uses a **CSS selector** to target elements.

```html
<script type="text/x-magento-init">
{
    "#my-element": {
        "Vendor_Module/js/myWidget": {
            "someOption": "customValue"
        }
    }
}
</script>
```

#### Using `*` for non-element-bound initialization

```html
<script type="text/x-magento-init">
{
    "*": {
        "Vendor_Module/js/myComponent": {
            "config": "value"
        }
    }
}
</script>
```

> The `"*"` selector initializes the component without binding it to any specific DOM element. Used for pure JavaScript components.

- **Exam focus:** `data-mage-init` is processed **per element** — the widget's `this.element` will be that specific DOM node. `x-magento-init` uses a **CSS selector** and can match multiple elements or dynamically loaded content.

---

## 4. `data-mage-init` vs `x-magento-init`

This comparison is **heavily tested**. Know every difference.

```
data-mage-init                      x-magento-init
+-----------------------------+     +-----------------------------+
| Lives on the HTML element   |     | Lives in a <script> tag     |
| as an attribute             |     | separate from target element |
+-----------------------------+     +-----------------------------+
| Widget gets THIS element    |     | Widget gets element(s)      |
| as this.element             |     | matched by CSS selector     |
+-----------------------------+     +-----------------------------+
| Element must exist in HTML  |     | Can target dynamically      |
| at parse time               |     | loaded/created elements     |
+-----------------------------+     +-----------------------------+
| JSON must use single quotes |     | JSON uses standard double   |
| as the outer wrapper        |     | quotes (inside <script>)    |
+-----------------------------+     +-----------------------------+
| One element, one widget     |     | One declaration can apply   |
| instance                    |     | to MANY matched elements    |
+-----------------------------+     +-----------------------------+
```

### Full Comparison Table

| Feature | `data-mage-init` | `x-magento-init` |
|---|---|---|
| Location | HTML attribute on target element | Separate `<script>` tag |
| Selector | Implicit (the element itself) | Explicit CSS selector string |
| Multiple elements | One per element | One declaration, many elements |
| Dynamic content | Requires element at render time | Works with dynamically injected elements |
| `this.element` | The element with the attribute | Element(s) matching the CSS selector |
| JSON quoting | Single quotes wrap JSON | Standard double-quoted JSON |
| `"*"` selector | N/A | Supported for non-DOM components |

### Code Comparison Side by Side

```html
<!-- data-mage-init: widget applied to THIS div -->
<div class="my-widget"
     data-mage-init='{"Vendor_Module/js/myWidget": {"color": "blue"}}'>
</div>

<!-- x-magento-init: widget applied to element(s) matching ".my-widget" -->
<div class="my-widget"></div>

<script type="text/x-magento-init">
{
    ".my-widget": {
        "Vendor_Module/js/myWidget": {"color": "blue"}
    }
}
</script>
```

- **Exam focus:** `x-magento-init` is **the preferred approach for widgets on dynamically created or AJAX-loaded content** because Magento re-scans for `x-magento-init` declarations when new content is injected into the DOM.
- **Exam focus:** Both `data-mage-init` and `x-magento-init` are processed by `mage/mage.js` during page initialization. Internally, `mage/apply/main` handles `data-mage-init` attributes, while `mage/apply/scripts` (loaded by main) handles `x-magento-init` script tags. Note: `Magento_Ui/js/core/app` is a **different** module — it handles UI component layout rendering, not widget initialization.

